---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import DocPage from '../../components/docs/DocPage.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/docs/ApiTable.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { SRC_URL, TENSOR_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Operator layer — DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'A validated density matrix, the Choi–Jamiołkowski isomorphism in both directions, the CPTP checks, and the dense complex linear algebra they are built from.';

const channel = `use deep_causality_quantum::{
    apply_choi, apply_kraus, check_completely_positive,
    check_trace_preserving, choi_from_kraus, kraus_from_choi,
};

// One channel, two representations.
let choi = choi_from_kraus(&kraus)?;

// A channel is legal iff both checks pass.
check_completely_positive(&choi, tol)?;         // J ⪰ 0
check_trace_preserving(&choi, d_in, d_out, tol)?; // Tr_out(J) = I_in

// Either route applies it, and the two agree.
let out_k = apply_kraus(&kraus, &rho)?;
let out_c = apply_choi(&choi, &rho, d_in, d_out)?;

// And the isomorphism runs backwards.
let recovered = kraus_from_choi(&choi, d_in, d_out, tol)?;`;

const trace = `// dims is row-major: leg 0 is most significant, and the product
// of dims must equal the matrix dimension.
let reduced = partial_trace(&op, &[d_a, d_b], &[1])?;  // the classic Tr_B`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/operators/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <DocPage
    eyebrow="Layer · Operators"
    title="The operator layer"
    lede="Everything above this layer assumes its operators are what they claim to be. This layer is where that gets checked."
    sourceHref={`${SRC_URL}/types/qgates`}
    sourceLabel="src/types/qgates"
  >
    <h2>Density matrices</h2>

    <p>
      <code>DensityMatrix&lt;R&gt;</code> holds a square complex matrix and its
      dimension. Every constructor enforces the same three invariants: Hermitian,
      positive semidefinite, unit trace. Positive semidefiniteness is decided
      through the dense Hermitian eigendecomposition supplied by
      <a href={TENSOR_URL}>deep_causality_tensor</a>, and the order matters,
      since that decomposition is only meaningful once Hermiticity holds.
    </p>

    <p>
      Four constructors cover the usual ways a state arrives.
      <code>new</code> validates a matrix at the default tolerance and
      <code>with_tolerance</code> at a supplied one. <code>from_ket</code> builds
      the pure state <code>|ψ⟩⟨ψ|</code> from a column vector.
      <code>from_ensemble</code> takes weighted kets and forms the convex
      mixture. <code>from_choi</code> reads a normalized state out of a Choi
      operator.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each violation has its own error rather than a shared one:
      <code>NonPositiveOperator</code> when an eigenvalue is negative beyond
      tolerance, <code>NonUnitTrace</code> when the trace misses 1, and
      <code>NonFiniteValue</code> when a NaN or an infinity is present. Reading
      the variant tells you which invariant broke without inspecting the matrix.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>purity()</code> returns <code>Tr(ρ²)</code>, and
      <code>is_pure(tol)</code> tests it against 1.
    </p>

    <h2>Channels</h2>

    <p>
      A quantum channel <code>E: L(H_in) → L(H_out)</code> has two standard
      representations, and the crate supports both plus the map between them.
      The Choi operator is
      <code>J(E) = Σ&#123;ik&#125; |i⟩⟨k| ⊗ E(|i⟩⟨k|)</code>, a square matrix of
      dimension <code>d_in · d_out</code> whose composite row index is
      <code>i·d_out + j</code>. For a Kraus family <code>&#123;K_κ&#125;</code>
      the entries are
      <code>J[(i,j),(k,l)] = Σ_κ K_κ[j,i]·conj(K_κ[l,k])</code>. Those
      conventions are fixed by the tests, not left to the reader.
    </p>

    <p>
      The two properties that make a map a channel are each a check on
      <code>J</code>. Complete positivity is <code>J ⪰ 0</code>; trace
      preservation is <code>Tr_out(J) = I_in</code>. Both are exposed as
      functions, so a caller can validate a channel it did not build.
    </p>

    <Code code={channel} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      <code>kraus_from_choi</code> is gated on finiteness and Hermiticity before
      it decomposes, for the same reason the density matrix is: the
      eigendecomposition it needs is only defined once those hold. A
      non-Hermitian input is refused rather than silently producing a family that
      reconstructs to something else.
    </p>

    <p>
      That the two application routes agree is a test, not an assumption.
      <code>test_apply_kraus_and_apply_choi_agree</code> in
      <code>channel_tests</code> holds the line, and it is also the Rust witness
      for the two LEAN linearity theorems about the reconstructed Choi action.
    </p>

    <h2>The dense kernels</h2>

    <p>
      Under the channel layer sits ordinary complex linear algebra on
      <code>CausalTensor&lt;Complex&lt;R&gt;&gt;</code>. Five of these are the
      ones the rest of the crate is built out of.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>partial_trace</code> traces out a named subset of tensor legs. It
      takes the per-leg dimensions and the legs to remove, rather than assuming a
      bipartition, so a model with several legs does not have to reshape first.
    </p>

    <Code code={trace} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      A non-square operator, a <code>dims</code> product that disagrees with the
      matrix dimension, and duplicate or out-of-range legs are all rejected, as
      <code>PartialTraceShape</code> or <code>DimensionMismatch</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>embed_on_legs</code> goes the other way. It lifts an operator from
      its own legs onto a larger labelled space, which is what puts two factors
      declared on different supports into the same matrix algebra so their
      commutator means something. The freeze check calls it on every pair it
      tests.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>matrix_commutator</code> forms <code>AB − BA</code>.
      <code>frobenius_norm</code> measures it. <code>hermiticity_defect</code>
      reports how far an operator is from Hermitian, and
      <code>identity_matrix</code> supplies the <code>I</code> that the
      trace-preservation check and the projection orthocomplement both need.
      <code>matrix_trace</code> and <code>supports_intersect</code> round out the
      set.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on the partial trace</h2>

    <p>
      Partial trace is linear and positive, and both facts are proved in LEAN.
      It is not an algebra homomorphism, and that is proved too, by
      counterexample. The distinction is not academic here: it is the reason the
      Markov check has to embed factors onto their common support rather than
      comparing reduced descriptions. The
      <a href="/formalization/">formalization page</a> gives the residual
      commutator explicitly.
    </p>
  </DocPage>

  <div class="wide-wrap">
    <ApiTable layer="operator" title="The operator surface" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wide-wrap {
    max-width: var(--w-panel);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-4) var(--space-9);
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) { .wide-wrap { padding: 0 var(--space-5) var(--space-9); } }
</style>
